@* Components/AppStepper.razor — shared step-nav strip (CLAUDE.md §3.1),
   wraps a list of AppStepItem. Split into two components (wrapper +
   item) rather than one monolithic file: this file owns layout/sequencing
   (the connector line, the CurrentIndex -> per-step State math), AppStepItem
   owns one step's own markup/state-to-class mapping — the two concerns
   don't overlap, and AppStepItem is independently reusable if a future
   screen ever needs a single step outside a full sequence.

   Renders as <ol> (a stepper genuinely IS an ordered sequence — native
   semantic HTML first, CLAUDE.md §6) inside a positioning shell <div>, since
   the connector is a set of absolutely-positioned elements behind every icon
   and "<div> directly inside <ol>" isn't valid HTML — they live in the
   shell, not the list.

   CONNECTOR — N-1 independent segments, not one spanning line (restructured
   this pass; was previously a single .app-stepper__connector div). Root
   cause for the change: re-scanning demo/ScreenB/TaskTwo_from_figma_design.png
   gap-by-gap (tokens.css's Stepper section has the full pixel history) found
   the real line is genuinely two-color — dark for a segment leading into a
   Pass/Active/Selected step, light gray for one leading into Disabled — which
   a single element with one background-color can never render. Each segment
   spans exactly from item i's icon-center to item i+1's icon-center via
   percentage math: with N equal-width flex items (.app-stepper__item,
   flex: 1 0 0) and each icon centered within its own item
   (justify-content: center), item i's icon-center sits at exactly
   (i + 0.5)/N of the row's inline size measured from the inline-start edge —
   the RTL row's right edge, where index 0 renders (this component's own
   established "first step renders at the RTL start" convention). Segment i
   therefore starts at (i + 0.5)/N and is exactly 1/N wide, landing its far
   edge on (i + 1.5)/N — item i+1's icon-center. See components.css's
   .app-stepper__connector-segment for the actual calc().

   Single horizontal row, not a wrapping grid: the isolated "Stepper btn"
   Figma component sheet shows several rows, but those are DEFAULT/HOVER/
   ONCLICK variant swatches for demonstration, not literal wrapped layout —
   confirmed against the real usage in
   demo/ScreenB/TaskTwo_from_figma_design.png, which is unambiguously one
   connected horizontal row (RTL: first step rightmost). No manual dir/rtl
   attribute is set here (CLAUDE.md §2 — only MainLayout's
   MudThemeProvider/MudRTLProvider set RightToLeft); the row simply inherits
   the app-wide RTL context and flexbox reverses direction on its own.

   No MudBlazor class is targeted anywhere in this component or AppStepItem
   — every rule lives in the shared styles/components.css +
   styles/responsive-rtl.css, not mudblazor-overrides.css or a .razor.css
   isolation file (CLAUDE.md §3.1: this defines a new reusable visual
   pattern, not one component's internal MudBlazor wiring). *@

<div class="app-stepper-shell">
    @for (var g = 0; g < Steps.Count - 1; g++)
    {
        var segmentIndex = g;
        var leadsIntoDisabled = GetState(segmentIndex + 1) == AppStepState.Disabled;
        <div class="@(leadsIntoDisabled ? "app-stepper__connector-segment app-stepper__connector-segment--disabled" : "app-stepper__connector-segment")"
             style="@($"--app-stepper-segment-index: {segmentIndex}; --app-stepper-segment-count: {Steps.Count}")"
             aria-hidden="true"></div>
    }
    <ol class="app-stepper" aria-label="@AriaLabel">
        @for (var i = 0; i < Steps.Count; i++)
        {
            var index = i;
            var step = Steps[index];
            <AppStepItem Icon="@step.Icon"
                         Label="@step.Label"
                         AlwaysShowLabel="@step.AlwaysShowLabel"
                         State="@GetState(index)"
                         OnClick="@(() => OnStepClick.InvokeAsync(index))" />
        }
    </ol>
</div>

@code {
    /// AppStepDefinition (Components/AppStepDefinition.cs), not a plain
    /// tuple — see that file's own doc comment for why (AlwaysShowLabel
    /// needs a name, not a third positional field every call site must spell
    /// out).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public List<AppStepDefinition> Steps { get; set; } = new();

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int CurrentIndex { get; set; }

    /// Overrides which index renders as Selected — defaults to
    /// CurrentIndex + 1 (the original, and still most common, "next step
    /// open to jump to" reading). ADDED for Screen B's real
    /// "تقسيم الأسئلة/الوحدات" screen (demo/ScreenB/TaskTwo_from_figma_design.png):
    /// pixel-sampled the actual rendered icon-circle fill colors there
    /// against the 4 confirmed --stepper-color-bg-* tokens (CLAUDE.md rule 1
    /// — not inferred from the page title or step order) and found Selected
    /// sitting BEFORE Active — one step's icon rendered the darkblue--15
    /// family, the very next one rendered the blue-00 family, the opposite
    /// order the CurrentIndex+1 formula assumes, and something no value of
    /// CurrentIndex alone can reproduce (this is an index-ORDER finding, not
    /// a color one — which of Active/Selected is styled darkblue vs. blue-00
    /// was later REVERSED in components.css/tokens.css per a later decision,
    /// see DECISIONS.md; that swap doesn't affect this override's own logic
    /// at all, since it operates purely on indices). Default value keeps every
    /// existing caller (StepperStatesGallery, and any future screen that
    /// never sets it) byte-for-byte identical to the prior hardcoded
    /// "index == CurrentIndex + 1" behavior — this is additive, not a
    /// redesign of the state model.
    [Parameter] public int? SelectedIndex { get; set; }

    /// Fires with the clicked step's index — only for Pass/Selected/Active
    /// (AppStepItem never fires OnClick for a Disabled step, see its own
    /// header comment), so no extra guard is needed here either.
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<int> OnStepClick { get; set; }

    /// Accessible name for the step list as a whole (e.g. "خطوات تقسيم
    /// الأسئلة") — screen-specific, so it's a required parameter rather than
    /// a hardcoded string here.
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// The confirmed real semantics (see tokens.css's Stepper section for
    /// the full history — this replaced an earlier 3-state guess):
    ///   index == CurrentIndex           -&gt; Active
    ///   index == EffectiveSelectedIndex -&gt; Selected  (default: CurrentIndex + 1)
    ///   index &lt; CurrentIndex            -&gt; Pass
    ///   otherwise                       -&gt; Disabled
    /// Active/Selected are checked before the Pass/Disabled split (rather
    /// than Pass being "index &lt; CurrentIndex" checked first) so a
    /// SelectedIndex override that lands BEFORE CurrentIndex — Screen B's
    /// real case, see SelectedIndex's own doc comment — still wins over the
    /// default Pass classification for that one index.
    private int EffectiveSelectedIndex => SelectedIndex ?? CurrentIndex + 1;

    private AppStepState GetState(int index)
    {
        if (index == CurrentIndex) return AppStepState.Active;
        if (index == EffectiveSelectedIndex) return AppStepState.Selected;
        if (index < CurrentIndex) return AppStepState.Pass;
        return AppStepState.Disabled;
    }
}
